<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Limpurg</h1><p class="st-meta">1230 CE – 1713 CE (approx.) · capital Gaildorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Franconian-Swabian lordship of the Schenks of Limpurg, hereditary imperial cupbearers, ruled from Gaildorf until the line died out in 1713.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schenk-von-limpurg">Schenks of Limpurg</a> were imperial ministerials who held the hereditary office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearer</a> of the Empire. Around 1230 they built Limpurg castle above <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-hall">Schwäbisch Hall</a>, and they acquired lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocher">Kocher</a> valley and the forests of the Limpurg uplands, forming a lordship held directly from the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship was rural, with forests, farms and small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaildorf">Gaildorf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obersontheim">Obersontheim</a>. Relations with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-hall">Free Imperial City of Schwäbisch Hall</a> were tense, and in 1541 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schenk-von-limpurg">Schenks</a> sold Limpurg castle to the city. The family adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and divided into branches with residences at Gaildorf and Sontheim. The imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearer</a> title gave them a ceremonial role at coronations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schenk-von-limpurg">Schenk</a> died in 1713, and the lands were divided among female heirs and their families, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> claiming part of the inheritance. The fragmented Limpurg lands were mediatised and passed to Württemberg in 1806.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1230 CE – 1713 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.18%;width:9.61%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1230 CE – 1713 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gaildorf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schenks of Limpurg, Limpurg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1472&amp;focus=lordship-of-limpurg" aria-label="Lordship of Limpurg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4906 468 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Limpurg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5226 724L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gaildorf. <a href="/atlas?year=1472&amp;focus=lordship-of-limpurg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

